    title: "Reading Passage",
    level: "C1",
    text: "Beyond the basic emotions lies a sophisticated landscape of complex feelings that require advanced vocabulary to express. Nostalgia is the bittersweet longing for the past — a warm ache that mixes happiness with sadness when recalling cherished memories. Ambivalence describes the state of holding contradictory feelings simultaneously: loving and resenting the same person, or wanting something while fearing it. Melancholy is a gentle, lingering sadness without a specific cause, often associated with artistic temperament and philosophical reflection. Resentment builds slowly — it is the quiet anger that simmers when we feel persistently wronged or overlooked. Envy and jealousy, though often confused, differ subtly: envy is wanting what someone else has, while jealousy is fearing the loss of what you already possess. Contempt is a cold mixture of anger and disgust directed at someone considered inferior or unworthy. Awe combines wonder, admiration, and sometimes fear — the feeling evoked by vast landscapes, extraordinary talent, or spiritual experiences. Compassion goes beyond empathy; it is feeling another's suffering coupled with the desire to help. Guilt arises from believing you have done something wrong, while shame is the deeper belief that you yourself are fundamentally flawed. Understanding these nuanced emotions enriches both self-awareness and the ability to connect deeply with others through language.",
